      "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c6eaea29-9ad0-5dcb-83aa-1fac36c8559a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1x+(1)y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,1)$,$(-1,3)$,$(-2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,1)}=1(3)+(1)(1)=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,3)}=1(-1)+(1)(3)=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=1(-2)+(1)(1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](59dd834cb5664b08e1eb4344692765033f15ccbe280b10a8cf5973facba54752.png)",
